ALVA — Add a How to Play Dialog

Objective

Make the existing question-mark (?) button in the top-right corner of the Home screen functional.

When clicked, it should open a polished How to Play dialog that briefly explains Alva’s core gameplay mechanics to new players.

The purpose is to help someone understand the fundamental rules of the game without overwhelming them with technical information or character-specific details.

Do not change any gameplay mechanics. This task is strictly about the Home interface and its help dialog.

1. Understand and reuse the existing architecture

Before implementing, inspect the relevant code, especially:

* js/screens/home-screen.js — Contains the currently disabled .home-help button.
* js/ui/overlays.js — Contains the existing InfoDialog and modal behavior.
* js/core/app.js — Manages dialog instances.
* css/overlays.css and css/home.css — Existing dialog and Home button styling.
* js/localization/strings/en.js and fr.js — English and French translations.
* docs/gameplay/battle.md — Elimination, scoring, and overtime.
* docs/systems/launch.md — Launch Point, Base Launch, and Directional Launch.
* docs/systems/energy.md and defense.md — Energy, Shield, and Deflect.

Reuse established UI patterns rather than introducing a separate design system.

2. Button behavior

The existing ? button should:

* Be enabled and clickable.
* Open the How to Play dialog without navigating away from Home.
* Remain in its current position alongside the other utility buttons.
* Support mouse, touch, keyboard, and controller navigation.
* Have an accessible label and dialog semantics.
* Return focus to the ? button when the dialog closes.

Keep its current icon and general appearance.

3. Dialog design

Use the existing Alva modal design language: dark translucent surfaces, subtle borders, restrained green accents, consistent typography, and the same visual styling as other information dialogs.

The dialog should have:

* The title How to Play.
* A close (×) button in the upper-right corner.
* Eight clearly separated sections.
* Short, readable paragraphs beneath their headings.
* Comfortable spacing without unnecessary decoration.
* A scrollable content area when necessary.

The dialog must work on desktop and mobile in landscape orientation, including smaller screens.

It should close using the close button, Escape, controller Back, or clicking outside the panel, consistent with existing dialogs.

Prefer extending or reusing InfoDialog to support section headings and descriptions. Preserve its existing behavior for other uses, including the Why Alva? dialog.

4. Exact English content

Use the following player-facing text as the content baseline. Keep the language natural, friendly, and concise.

How to Play

Elimination

There’s no health bar in Alva. Instead, you score a point whenever your opponent falls into the Void. The first to reach 3 points wins the match.

Launch Point

Every unblocked hit raises your Launch Point, making you fly farther when launched. It gradually decreases when you go without taking hits.

Base Launch

Base Launch determines how strongly a hit sends someone flying. The higher its value, the stronger the launch becomes, especially against someone with a high Launch Point. A Base Launch of 0 doesn’t send anyone flying.

Directional Launch

Not every hit sends you in the same direction. Depending on the attack, you can be launched sideways, upward, or downward.

Overtime

If both players have the same score when the 7-minute timer runs out, a 1-minute overtime begins. The Void gradually closes in, making it harder to stay safe. If the score is still tied when overtime ends, the player with the lower Launch Point wins. If both are equal, the match ends in a tie.

Energy

Energy is used for dashing, Deflecting, and blocking hits with your Shield. It refills automatically, but if it runs out completely, you can’t use those actions again until it’s fully restored.

Shield

Hold the Shield button while on the ground to block incoming attacks from any direction. Keeping your Shield raised doesn’t cost Energy, but each hit it blocks does.

Deflect

While in the air, press the Shield button to perform a Deflect instead. It can strike nearby opponents and send incoming projectiles back toward their source. Deflecting costs Energy, even if it misses.

5. Writing requirements

The How to Play dialog must remain a brief introduction, not a complete gameplay manual.

Strict content rules:

* Cover only the eight sections listed above.
* Never mention specific characters, character names, fighter IDs, individual moves, special abilities, or character-specific statistics.
* Don’t introduce additional sections about controls, combos, movement, game modes, settings, or advanced combat systems.
* Avoid internal development terminology, code references, formulas, or technical explanations in the player-facing content.
* Use everyday language that someone playing for the first time can immediately understand.
* Keep the eight section headings visible and easy to scan.
* Avoid redundant explanations.
* Preserve the actual rules documented in the repository.

The reader should understand why eliminations matter, how launching works, when overtime occurs, and how Energy, Shield, and Deflect interact.

Don’t make the text unnecessarily long.

6. Localization

Alva supports both English and French.

Integrate the How to Play text into the existing localization system.

* Add translation keys for the dialog title, eight headings, and eight descriptions.
* Provide natural French translations that preserve the meaning and approachable tone of the English copy.
* Ensure the dialog displays the language selected in Settings.
* Avoid hardcoding visible text directly into JavaScript components.
* Switching the interface language must not leave stale translations in the dialog.

Do not change existing translations unrelated to this feature.

7. Technical implementation

Follow the repository’s existing conventions.

Important requirements:

* Do not install new dependencies.
* Do not modify the underlying combat, scoring, physics, or defense systems.
* Do not introduce duplicate modal infrastructure when existing utilities can handle the task.
* Preserve existing overlay behavior, focus management, and controller navigation.
* Keep styling consistent with the current Home interface.
* Ensure the dialog content remains scrollable and readable on small screens.
* Keep the implementation maintainable and avoid unnecessary abstractions.

If structured headings require extending InfoDialog, make that extension optional and backward-compatible.

8. Validation

Verify the following:

1. The ? button is no longer disabled.
2. Clicking it opens How to Play.
3. All eight sections appear in the correct order.
4. No other gameplay subjects appear.
5. No specific characters or abilities are mentioned.
6. The dialog closes correctly through supported methods.
7. Keyboard and controller navigation work.
8. Focus returns to the Help button after closing.
9. English and French content display correctly.
10. The content scrolls correctly on smaller screens.
11. Other dialogs continue functioning as before.
12. Existing tests pass using node --test.

Add or update appropriate interface tests for the new behavior.

Final expectation

The result should feel like a native part of Alva: a small, accessible, well-designed help dialog that explains the essential gameplay rules in a way a new player can read and understand quickly.

Prioritize clarity, simplicity, correctness, and consistency with the existing interface.

Implement the feature completely, without unrelated changes.
